00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.270 align:start position:0%
hospitals have been repeatedly bombed in  what has been described as a systematic  campaign  to force the opposition to surrender  hundreds of airstrikes by syrian  government and russian warplanes  have been documented throughout the  years of conflict  many advocacy groups like physicians for  human rights  have tracked at least 595 air or  artillery attacks  on health care facilities in syria since  2011.  two years later they were described as  war crimes by u.n investigators  who said the hospitals were being bombed  to gain  military advantage but apart from  condemnations  little else was done and the rules of  war  continued to be ignored  the russian and syrian governments have  denied targeting civilians or civilian  buildings  despite evidence to the contrary and  international recognition of the  widespread and sometimes deliberate  nature  of the attacks the health sector has  been  decimated we know in northwest syria for  example the un has said that  50 percent of health facilities are no  longer functioning we know that 70  percent of health workers  have fled the country or been killed a  survey by the international rescue  committee showed  the risks health workers and civilians  faced in the past decade  81 percent said they had a co-worker or  patient injured  or killed 77 percent witnessed an  average of four attacks  on health care facilities eight percent  were  inside a health facility when it was  attacked  two of the hospitals i was working in  was hit  i survived but the feeling is hard to  describe  we all felt vulnerable we all knew the  risks of being in a hospital  it meant we could be killed  a decade of attacks has also compromised  the health care system  and its ability to respond to the needs  of millions of syrians  in opposition controlled areas in the  north and the effects of the coronavirus  pandemic are an added pressure  on an already weak system where there is  one doctor for every ten  thousand civilians the situation is not  good  there are millions of people in idlib in  the countryside of aleppo  no matter how much aid we get it's just  not enough  we really suffer from a lack of  medicines and medical equipment to treat  chronic diseases  a ceasefire appears to be largely  holding but it hasn't eased the  humanitarian crisis  the violence has caused critical damage  to syria's health  sector affecting the ability of millions  to receive  proper health care senator al jazeera  beirut
 

